3 + 9x^2 + ax - 30,$ where $a$ and $b$ are real numbers, find $a + b.$","role":"user"}],"rendered_sha256":"c64450c008b7e15afe1295c5cba464b0c2ceb190cc0baeb3798b4a07cebc0a43"},"seconds":14.646528959274292,"seed":1141232931002536679,"stop":"eos","text":"Given that both \\(i - 3\\) and \\(b\\) are roots of the polynomial \\(ax^3 + 9x^2 + ax - 30\\), and knowing that the coefficients are real, the complex roots must occur in conjugate pairs. Therefore, \\(-i - 3\\) is also a root of the polynomial.\n\nThe polynomial can be expressed as:\n\\[a(x - (i - 3))(x - (-i - 3))(x - b) = a(x - i + 3)(x + i + 3)(x - b)\\]\n\nFirst, we simplify the product of the complex conjugate factors:\n\\[(x - i + 3)(x + i + 3) = (x + 3 - i)(x + 3 + i) = (x + 3)^2 - i^2 = (x + 3)^2 + 1 = x^2 + 6x + 9 + 1 = x^2 + 6x + 10\\]\n\nSo the polynomial becomes:\n\\[a(x^2 + 6x + 10)(x - b)\\]\n\nExpanding this product, we get:\n\\[a(x^3 - bx^2 + 6x^2 - 6bx + 10x - 10b) = a(x^3 + (6 - b)x^2 + (10 - 6b)x - 10b)\\]\n\nWe know this polynomial is equal to \\(ax^3 + 9x^2 + ax - 30\\). By comparing coefficients, we get:\n\\[a = a\\]\n\\[a(6 - b) = 9\\]\n\\[a(10 - 6b) = a\\]\n\\[-10ab = -30\\]\n\nFrom the fourth equation, we can solve for \\(b\\):\n\\[-10ab = -30 \\implies ab = 3 \\implies b = \\frac{3}{a}\\]\n\nSubstituting \\(b = \\frac{3}{a}\\) into the second equation:\n\\[a\\left(6 - \\frac{3}{a}\\right) = 9 \\implies 6a - 3 = 9 \\implies 6a = 12 \\implies a = 2\\]\n\nThen, substituting \\(a = 2\\) back into \\(b = \\frac{3}{a}\\):\n\\[b = \\frac{3}{2}\\]\n\nTherefore, \\(a + b = 2 + \\frac{3}{2} = \\frac{4}{2} + \\frac{3}{2} = \\frac{7}{2}\\).\n\nThe final answer is:\n\\[\\boxed{\\frac{7}{2}}\\]"},"grade":{"answer":"\\boxed{\\frac{7}{2}}","correct":true,"format":"boxed","parseable":true},"id":"intermediate_algebra:430"}
